1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:11,460
In this video we'll show you all of the options you have for working with your scan results

2
00:00:11,460 --> 00:00:14,040
in Burp Suite Enterprise Edition.

3
00:00:14,040 --> 00:00:17,800
Regardless of where you are, you can always select Scans from the top navigation menu

4
00:00:17,800 --> 00:00:21,900
to view a list of all of the scans that are currently scheduled, running, completed or

5
00:00:21,900 --> 00:00:25,820
even that have failed in your system.

6
00:00:25,820 --> 00:00:30,560
You can then drill down into a particular scan to see more detailed information about it.

7
00:00:30,560 --> 00:00:35,120
The kind of information that you'll see here really depends on the scan's status.

8
00:00:35,120 --> 00:00:39,320
For example, if you click on a scan that's scheduled but hasn't actually started yet,

9
00:00:39,320 --> 00:00:44,060
you can just view and edit some basic details, such as which scan configurations will be applied

10
00:00:44,060 --> 00:00:46,580
and when the scan will run.

11
00:00:46,580 --> 00:00:51,300
For recurring scans you can also see a list of any previous occurrences of this scan.

12
00:00:51,300 --> 00:00:54,320
But you'll mostly be interested in looking at scans that have already finished, so you

13
00:00:54,320 --> 00:00:58,180
can start working with the results.

14
00:00:58,180 --> 00:01:02,700
As you can see here, the initial overview tab gives you a snapshot of key information about

15
00:01:02,700 --> 00:01:06,120
the vulnerabilities that have been identified by this scan.

16
00:01:06,120 --> 00:01:09,860
In the case of recurring scans, you can also get an idea of whether the number of issues

17
00:01:09,860 --> 00:01:14,400
has increased or decreased for this site over time.

18
00:01:14,400 --> 00:01:20,040
And just a small tip, if you ever want to include any of these charts in presentations or reports,

19
00:01:20,040 --> 00:01:25,900
for example, you can just click this icon here to download them as either a JPEG or PNG.

20
00:01:25,900 --> 00:01:31,900
All of the issues found by scans are given both a severity and confidence rating.

21
00:01:31,900 --> 00:01:37,900
High severity issues are any vulnerabilities that generally pose a greater security risk.

22
00:01:37,900 --> 00:01:42,300
That said, please keep in mind that this is just an approximate guide.

23
00:01:42,300 --> 00:01:46,860
The impact of a particular issue can vary quite a lot between different websites and may depend

24
00:01:46,860 --> 00:01:49,360
on the type of functionality in which it occurs.

25
00:01:49,360 --> 00:01:53,060
So you should always take into account your own knowledge of the application and functionality

26
00:01:53,060 --> 00:01:58,200
when trying to determine the severity of an issue that's been found.

27
00:01:58,200 --> 00:02:02,600
The confidence level is just a reflection of how confident Burp Scanner is that this vulnerability

28
00:02:02,600 --> 00:02:04,580
is actually present.

29
00:02:04,580 --> 00:02:09,720
This mainly depends on how Burp Scanner checks for different types of vulnerability.

30
00:02:09,720 --> 00:02:14,260
Detecting some vulnerabilities, such as reflected XSS for example, is fairly straightforward,

31
00:02:14,260 --> 00:02:18,900
whereas others may rely on techniques that are inherently more prone to false positives.

32
00:02:18,900 --> 00:02:23,340
Any issues that have a confidence rating lower than certain will require some level of manual

33
00:02:23,340 --> 00:02:26,260
review to confirm.

34
00:02:26,260 --> 00:02:30,500
If you go to the issues tab, you can see all of the issues that have been found by this

35
00:02:30,500 --> 00:02:31,940
scan.

36
00:02:31,940 --> 00:02:35,760
Or in the case of a scan that's currently running, this will show all of the issues that have

37
00:02:35,760 --> 00:02:42,200
been found so far, and these are updated in real time as the scan progresses.

38
00:02:42,200 --> 00:02:47,060
You have a few options here for filtering the list based on the severity and confidence levels,

39
00:02:47,060 --> 00:02:51,780
and you can also choose to show only new or regressed issues.

40
00:02:51,780 --> 00:02:57,140
A new issue is one that was found by the current scan that was not present in any previous scans

41
00:02:57,140 --> 00:02:59,760
of this site.

42
00:02:59,760 --> 00:03:04,700
A regressed issue is any issue that was found by a previous scan, which initially appeared

43
00:03:04,700 --> 00:03:11,040
to have been resolved, but has now reappeared in the most recent scan of the site.

44
00:03:11,040 --> 00:03:15,020
If you click on any of these issues, you'll see that we provide some basic background information

45
00:03:15,020 --> 00:03:18,900
about what exactly the issue is, as well as some general remediation advice to help you

46
00:03:18,900 --> 00:03:20,080
fix it.

47
00:03:20,080 --> 00:03:24,440
In some cases, there are also links to additional resources where you can read up on the vulnerability

48
00:03:24,440 --> 00:03:27,800
in more detail if you're interested.

49
00:03:27,800 --> 00:03:31,540
You'll also see some additional tabs that contain evidence that BurtScanner has found

50
00:03:31,540 --> 00:03:33,080
for this issue.

51
00:03:33,080 --> 00:03:36,640
These will differ depending on the type of issue that you're looking at.

52
00:03:36,640 --> 00:03:42,220
In this case, you can see the HTTP request that was sent, including the proof of concept payload,

53
00:03:42,220 --> 00:03:44,720
which is highlighted in red.

54
00:03:44,720 --> 00:03:49,960
This is then followed by the response, which shows the same payload being reflected.

55
00:03:49,960 --> 00:03:55,000
After closer inspection, you might decide that a particular issue is in fact a false positive,

56
00:03:55,000 --> 00:03:58,760
and you can mark it as such by clicking this button here.

57
00:03:58,760 --> 00:04:03,300
You'll then present it with different options for mass applying the false positive flag to

58
00:04:03,300 --> 00:04:05,360
similar issues on the site.

59
00:04:05,360 --> 00:04:10,700
In this case, I'll just mark this specific instance as a false positive.

60
00:04:10,700 --> 00:04:14,200
If you look at the list of issues on the left here, you can see that this one is now greyed

61
00:04:14,200 --> 00:04:15,820
out.

62
00:04:15,820 --> 00:04:19,160
False positives will also be excluded from any of the charts that are displayed on the

63
00:04:19,160 --> 00:04:21,400
various dashboards.

64
00:04:21,400 --> 00:04:25,860
If your administrator has configured a connection to JIRA, you might also have the option to create

65
00:04:25,860 --> 00:04:28,580
a JIRA ticket directly from the issue.

66
00:04:28,580 --> 00:04:32,220
In that case, there would be an additional button here in the action section to allow you to

67
00:04:32,220 --> 00:04:34,400
do this.

68
00:04:34,400 --> 00:04:37,660
You may also find it useful to look at the scanned URLs tab.

69
00:04:37,660 --> 00:04:41,580
This shows you all of the endpoints that the scan attempted to crawl and audit, and provides

70
00:04:41,580 --> 00:04:47,060
information about the issues that were found and any errors that occurred at specific URLs.

71
00:04:47,060 --> 00:04:53,320
You can toggle between a basic list view or a hierarchical tree structure.

72
00:04:53,320 --> 00:04:57,780
Both the scan and site details tabs should be fairly self-explanatory.

73
00:04:57,780 --> 00:05:01,940
One thing to point out is that the site details tab shows the site's configuration at the

74
00:05:01,940 --> 00:05:08,000
time of the scan, which might not be the same as the site's current configuration.

75
00:05:08,000 --> 00:05:13,940
On the reporting and logs tab, you can generate different types of scan report in HTML format.

76
00:05:13,940 --> 00:05:18,360
This is especially useful for sharing the results of your scans with colleagues or other stakeholders

77
00:05:18,360 --> 00:05:23,480
who might not necessarily have access to Burpsuite Enterprise Edition themselves.

78
00:05:23,480 --> 00:05:27,160
If you find that a scan keeps running into problems, it can be helpful to download the

79
00:05:27,160 --> 00:05:28,780
scan event log.

80
00:05:28,780 --> 00:05:32,380
This might shed some light on why the scan's failing, or at least tell you which phase of

81
00:05:32,380 --> 00:05:34,940
the scan is unable to run.

82
00:05:34,940 --> 00:05:38,680
Finally, you've got the option to download the scan debug log.

83
00:05:38,680 --> 00:05:41,760
This won't be of much use to you directly, but if you ever need to raise a ticket with

84
00:05:41,760 --> 00:05:44,660
our support team, they might ask you to provide this.

85
00:05:44,660 --> 00:05:51,840
It contains some basic information that we can use to help you with your problem more efficiently.

86
00:05:51,840 --> 00:05:55,680
And just to wrap up, I just want to show you that if you drill into a particular site once

87
00:05:55,680 --> 00:05:59,240
you've run a few scans on it, you'll notice that some of the tabs that were previously

88
00:05:59,240 --> 00:06:02,300
empty are now populated with some useful data.

89
00:06:02,300 --> 00:06:06,400
The dashboard shows you some more charts about the security posture of the site, and as the

90
00:06:06,400 --> 00:06:10,600
names suggest, the scans and issues tabs show you all of the scans and issues for this

91
00:06:10,600 --> 00:06:12,500
particular site.

92
00:06:12,500 --> 00:06:16,740
If you've created any folders, you can also select these to see aggregated data about all

93
00:06:16,740 --> 00:06:19,180
of the sites within that folder.

94
00:06:19,180 --> 00:06:22,760
So depending on how you've decided to organise your folders, this can be really useful for

95
00:06:22,760 --> 00:06:26,820
monitoring things like how different teams are getting on with resolving issues, or tracking

96
00:06:26,820 --> 00:06:31,120
the security posture of a particular region over time.

97
00:06:31,120 --> 00:06:34,660
So that's everything you need to know to get started working with scan results in Burpsuite

98
00:06:34,660 --> 00:06:35,540
Enterprise Edition.

